\paragraph{Default assignment artifact.} The apparent \cwm{} accuracy increase from 4B to 8B is an artifact of rising unparseable rate (53.7\%$\to$73.8\%), inflating accuracy via majority-class default while parseable-only accuracy decreases (42.88\%$\to$38.52\%); cross-seed evidence confirms the pattern (Appendix~\ref{app:parseable_bias}).

\paragraph{Complexity as moderator.} The \cls{}--\cwm{} gap widens from 1--3\pp{} (function) to 18--26\pp{} (repo) under our protocol; a matched-context ablation confirms input disparity explains $<$3\% of this gap (Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}).

\paragraph{Formulation$\times$complexity interaction.} A mixed-effects LPM ($n{=}56{,}480$, 139 clusters, DeepSeek-Coder) confirms: \cwm{} effect at function level is negligible ($\hat{\beta}{=}-0.002$, $p{=}0.805$), but the formulation$\times$complexity interaction is highly significant ($z{=}-23.7$, $p{<}0.001$). A cross-model LPM confirms formulation dominates ($\hat{\beta}{=}-0.22$ vs.\ model $\hat{\beta}{=}+0.011$); model identity significance is estimator-dependent (LPM $p{=}0.002$; GEE $p{=}0.449$), consistent with limited clusters ($k{=}25 < 40$; Appendix~\ref{app:interaction}).

\subsection{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{Aggregation penalty.} The aggregation penalty ranges from 1.36\pp{} to 19\pp{}, negligible at function level ($\bar{T} \approx 2.3$) but substantial at repo level ($\bar{T} \gg 10$; Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}(a)).

\paragraph{Why does generation degrade at repo level?} Output complexity increases nonlinearly: function-level outputs average ${\sim}$5--15 tokens; repo-level outputs average ${\sim}$477 tokens, causing format collapse (53--74\% unparseable). Even parseable outputs achieve only 38--43\%, below majority; forced parsing confirms the bottleneck is semantic, not syntactic (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}(c)). Four targeted interventions all fail to close this gap: doubling LoRA rank to~32 does not improve format compliance; majority voting ($k{=}5$) is ineffective because collapsed outputs are near-identical across samples; relaxed matching still leaves ${>}$50\% unparseable; and scaling from 4B to 8B worsens the unparseable rate from 53.7\% to 73.8\% (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}(g)). Checkpoint selection does not explain the gap: matched epoch-3 comparisons yield 20--23\pp{} gaps, and \cls{} varies $<$1\pp{} across epochs (Appendix~\ref{app:cwm_convergence}). \cls{} achieves 217$\times$ higher throughput than \cwm{} (1 vs.\ avg 477 output tokens; Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}(i)).

\paragraph{Error complementarity.} \cls{} and \cwm{} exhibit partially complementary errors. An oracle combination achieves 90.63\% (+2.80\pp{}; 95\% CI [89.3\%, 91.9\%]; single-seed) at function level and 95.83\% (+14.58\pp{}; $n{=}48$) at repo level. Simple routing heuristics capture $<$0.4\pp{} of the oracle gap; the complementarity is instance-specific, suggesting a learned router is needed (Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}).

\subsection{Threats to Validity}
\label{sec:threats}

We identify five potential confounds (Appendix~\ref{app:threats}): checkpoint selection ($<$1\pp{}), evaluation sequence length (${\sim}$1.5\pp{}), preprocessing (${\sim}$8\pp{}), \traj{} training regime, and \cwm{} scale/pretraining (primary limitation). Preprocessing and checkpoint interact, accounting for up to 7.68\pp{} variation within one model (Appendix~\ref{app:threats}). The 18--26\pp{} gap exceeds any single confound, but correlated confounds and limited seeds preclude definitive causal attribution. Holm-Bonferroni ($k{=}5$) confirms the two core claims (adjusted $p{=}0.015$ and $p{=}0.028$; Table~\ref{tab:holm}).

