\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented the first systematic comparison of three execution-free verification formulations (\cls{}, \cwm{}, \traj{}). At function level, \cls{} and \cwm{} are statistically equivalent (TOST $p{=}0.007$, 5 seeds); \traj{} equivalence is not confirmed after correction, but the gap narrows monotonically to 1.07\pp{} at 14B. At repository level under ${\leq}$8B LoRA, divergence reaches 18--26\pp{} ($z{=}-23.7$), consistent across families (\S\ref{sec:threats}). Preliminary 14B results (3 seeds, single family) narrow all gaps to within 1.07\pp{}, though formal confirmation requires more seeds. This contrasts with mathematical reasoning, where generative verification outperforms discriminative~\cite{hosseini2025genrm}; we attribute this to output structure---code verification requires exact-match outputs (${\sim}$477 tokens/test for \cwm{} vs.\ 1 for \cls{}), and $>$50\% of repo-level \cwm{} outputs are unparseable, converting generation from a reasoning advantage into a format fidelity challenge absent in math.

Three directions follow: (1)~complexity-aware routing; (2)~scaling to 32B+ with more seeds~\cite{meta2025cwm}; (3)~per-test diagnostics for downstream repair.

\section*{Limitations}

Primary findings use ${\leq}$8B LoRA fine-tuning with multi-seed replication; 14B results use a single model family (Qwen3) with 3 seeds; cross-family replication and evaluation at 32B+ are needed. The repo-level gap may diminish at 32B+ scale or with execution-trace pretraining~\cite{meta2025cwm}. All evaluation uses Python-only benchmarks; other languages may exhibit different format compliance rates. Training data uses synthetic mutation operators~\cite{andrews2005mutation,just2014mutants}; the synthetic-to-real transfer gap remains untested (Appendix~\ref{app:limitations}).
